  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It typechecks, but I couldn't run it in a browser, so none of it has been run or looked at. That includes the physics, the camera framing and the DOM label positions.\n\n- **Scene:** The board is 8 wide, with 10 rows of pegs alternating 6 and 7 per row. There are 7 equal bins under it, separated by dividers. A key light and a cyan rim light plus a neutral environment light it. Pegs are metallic, balls are glossy orange, and walls, dividers and bin backs use separate matte materials. The camera is a fixed front view meant to fit the whole board and bins.\n- **Physics:** I wrote my own 2D rigid-circle simulation instead of using the engine's physics module. It runs at a fixed 1/240 s step with gravity 9.81, restitution 0.4, and ball-ball, ball-peg, divider, wall and floor collisions. The result is repeatable.\n- **Drop:** \"Drop 20 Balls\" spawns ball i at i × 0.25 simulated seconds, at x = ((i×37)%11 − 5)×0.02. If balls from an earlier drop are still on the board, Drop clears them and starts again. It does nothing while a drop is in progress. \"Reset\" clears all balls and counts.\n- **Bin counts:** A ball counts toward a bin once its centre is below the divider tops, whether or not it has stopped moving.\n- **Numbers and counts:** The bin numbers 1–7 and their live counts are DOM labels below the bins. They are placed by projecting bin positions with the camera's vertical fov and assuming the 640×360 canvas, so they could sit slightly off if the engine frames differently.\n- **State and ready flag:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only getter, updated every frame. Phase is `ready` until a drop starts, then `dropping`, then `settled` once all 20 are in bins and slow. `__EVAL_READY__` is set on the first rendered frame.",
